<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baal Hammon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ba'al Hammon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Hammon was the chief god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Punic world, worshipped with the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a>. His sanctuaries, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tophet">tophets</a>, contain urns of burnt infants and animals whose interpretation as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a> is still debated. After the Roman conquest his cult continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as that of Saturn, the leading god of the African countryside until the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baal-hammon.jpg" alt="Baal Hammon" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_statue_of_Baal-Hammon_on_a_throne_AvL.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Baal Hammon, whose name means Lord Hammon, was a weather god held responsible for the fertility of vegetation and esteemed as king of the gods. He was shown as a bearded older man with curling ram's horns. His worship was confined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, including Numidia, and to the Carthaginian colonies of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>. Many scholars connect the second part of his name with Ammon, the god of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> whose oracle stood in the Siwa Oasis, and describe him as a deity of mixed Punic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> character.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> he was especially associated with the ram and was also worshipped as Baal Qarnaim, Lord of the Two Horns, in an open-air sanctuary at Jebel Boukornine, the two-horned hill across the bay from Carthage. He had temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, Iol, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timgad">Timgad</a> and many other places, and 178 temples of his have been listed. From the 5th century BCE his consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanit">Tanit</a> came to eclipse him and was often named before him on monuments.</p><p>Greek and Roman writers recorded that he was propitiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/child-sacrifice">child sacrifice</a>, probably in times of crisis or by the elite. Scholars long dismissed this as propaganda until archaeologists found urns holding the cremated remains of infants at ritual sites; some take them as confirmation, others as the remains of children who died young. Scholars have also identified him variously with a solar god, a moon god (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yigael-yadin">Yigael Yadin</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a> (Edward Lipiński).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified him with the Titan Cronus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> with Saturn; exchange between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> may have shaped the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia">Saturnalia</a>. Dedications and art of the Severan era show a revival of the cult in Roman North Africa. Traces survive in the region: Algerians and Tunisians call non-irrigated farming Baali farming, some Tunisian first names are built on his name, a street near the Punic Ports of modern Carthage bears it, and the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmona">Carmona</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> is believed to derive from Kar-Hammon, city of Hammon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baal Hammon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saturn, the Roman form of the Punic <b>Baal Hammon</b>, was the leading god of the countryside before Christianity spread rapidly in the second and third centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion blended Berber cults with Punic gods such as <b>Baal Hammon</b> and Tanit, and the kings themselves received cult after death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later account of Philo of Byblos, said to come from Sanchuniathon of Berytus, the creator Elion was father of the gods and married Beruth, “the city”, as other gods were linked with theirs: Melqart with Tyre, Chemosh with Moab, Tanit and <b>Baal Hammon</b> with Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
